(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with 20 years of type 2 diabetes has fainted twice shortly after getting out of bed. Her mucous membranes are moist, and vibration and light-touch sensation are reduced in both feet. Supine she is 140/80 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 110/66 with a heart rate of 74. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasovagal reflex syncope","Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The systolic fall of 30 mmHg meets the criterion, the heart rate barely rises, and long-standing diabetes with neuropathy points to diabetic autonomic neuropathy. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with exertional chest tightness develops T-wave inversion during an exercise stress test. Which test is the gold standard for confirming coronary artery disease?","choices":["Transthoracic echocardiogram","Nuclear stress test","Coronary calcium score","Coronary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography, with dye injected at catheterization and pictures taken of the coronary arteries, is the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease after a positive stress test. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 38"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has sudden severe breathlessness, crackles and edema. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 1,150. How should this level be read?","choices":["The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is ruled out","Only chronic heart failure fits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Above 900 acute heart failure is likely. Above 400 heart failure is likely in general, 100 to 400 is indeterminate, and below 100 rules acute failure out. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, breathlessness with exertion and no symptoms at rest. He takes furosemide and sacubitril-valsartan. Which drug class should be added as core therapy?","choices":["Calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Milrinone as an outpatient infusion","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reduced ejection fraction carries strong recommendations for a beta blocker, renin-angiotensin blockade, a mineralocorticoid antagonist and a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor; he has only the renin-angiotensin piece so far. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which outcome is associated with consuming fatty fish or fish-oil supplements?","choices":["Higher high-density lipoprotein than niacin","More low-density lowering than statins","Cure of familial hypercholesterolemia","Lower risk of coronary death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fatty fish or fish-oil supplements are associated with lower risk of coronary heart disease and coronary death, may lower thrombotic stroke risk, and at least 2 servings of fish a week are advised. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has increasing lethargy, episodes of near-syncope and cool extremities, without new edema. Which feature of the heart failure syndrome do these findings reflect?","choices":["Worsening pulmonary congestion","Inadequate tissue perfusion","Systemic volume overload","Portal venous overload"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Weakness, fatigue, lethargy, malaise, syncope or near-syncope and obtundation are signs of inadequate tissue perfusion, the low-output side of the syndrome, distinct from congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes fainted after walking to the bathroom. Supine she is 146/84 with a heart rate of 70; after 3 minutes standing she is 118/70 with a heart rate of 72. What does her heart rate response indicate?","choices":["The autonomic response is intact","An arrhythmia is present","The autonomic response has failed","Volume depletion is the cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pressure fall with only a minimal heart rate rise means the sympathetic reflex is not compensating, which favors neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman's previously well-controlled blood pressure has risen over two months. She denies missing doses. What should the medication review specifically cover beyond her prescriptions?","choices":["Only agents started within the past week","Only her antihypertensive agents","Only intravenous medications given in hospital","Over-the-counter drugs, licorice and supplements"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and patients frequently do not volunteer any of these because they do not regard them as medication. A sudden loss of previously stable control is itself a clue to a secondary cause. Recreational substances belong in the same review. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man arrives for his first adult screening visit, having eaten lunch an hour ago. Which lipid test can be done today without fasting?","choices":["Total and high-density cholesterol","Triglyceride and total cholesterol","Calculated low-density lipoprotein","Triglyceride with high-density cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Total and high-density cholesterol need no fasting; their ratio is the most efficient and cost-effective way to predict coronary risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with untreated severe aortic stenosis now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and ankle swelling. Without valve replacement, which average survival is expected from this symptom?","choices":["About 10 years","About 3 years","About 5 years","About 2 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Of the cardinal symptoms, heart failure or exertional dyspnea is the most common and carries the worst prognosis, about 2 years. Angina carries about 5 years and syncope about 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 16, 22"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with a triglyceride of 380 mg/dL is already on a moderate-intensity statin and at his low-density goal. Which drug is the most effective choice to add for his triglyceride?","choices":["Alirocumab","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Fenofibrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates such as fenofibrate are the most effective for lowering triglyceride, by 20% to 50%, and they also raise high-density lipoprotein 10% to 30%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with hypertensive retinopathy achieves good blood pressure control. What can be expected of his retinal findings?","choices":["The changes have no relationship to blood pressure","The changes will progress regardless of control","Control can lead to regression of the retinopathy","The changes are permanent once established"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so the finding is both a prognostic marker and something that can improve with treatment. Retinopathy predicts long-term stroke risk independently of the pressure level. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, is class II on optimal guideline-directed therapy, and has metastatic cancer with an expected survival of a few months. Which is most appropriate regarding an implantable defibrillator?","choices":["Implant it once he is class IV","Implant it before discharge","Implant it after a cardiac arrest","It is not indicated for him"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The device is indicated only with a life expectancy of at least one year, together with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III and optimal therapy. His expected survival of months fails that criterion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is found to have a raised creatinine. What is required before chronic kidney disease can be diagnosed?","choices":["Structural renal disease demonstrated on imaging","A kidney abnormality persisting three months","A single raised creatinine measurement","Albuminuria alone, regardless of duration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A single raised value may reflect a reversible insult, so duration is part of the definition rather than an optional confirmation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"For reflex hypotension, what is the treatment principle?","choices":["Treat the autonomic disease","Reduce or remove the medication","Start a vasopressor infusion","Reduce or remove the trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The principle is to remove the offending agent or trigger: reflex causes are managed by reducing or removing the trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has severe mitral regurgitation with symptoms of left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Her ejection fraction is 52% and the left ventricular end-systolic dimension is 45 mm. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","A loop diuretic and yearly echo","A mitral valve clip or replacement","Aortic valve replacement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is indicated for severe mitral regurgitation with symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction, an ejection fraction below 60% or an end-systolic dimension above 40 mm. Options include a mitral valve clip or transcatheter mitral valve replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man is newly found to have heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with low-density lipoprotein of 250 mg/dL. He has read about a new injectable and wants it instead of any pill. Which option is inappropriate as initial therapy?","choices":["Addressing smoking and blood pressure","A high-intensity statin","A low saturated-fat, low-cholesterol diet","Evolocumab before trying a statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The steps must be followed: high-intensity statins first, then ezetimibe and nicotinic acid, and the injectable only for patients refractory to drug therapy. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with heart failure diagnosed three years ago has had four days of worsening orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg swelling. How is this episode classified?","choices":["Acute on chronic heart failure","Acute new-onset heart failure","High-output heart failure","Pre-heart failure, stage B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure present for more than a year is chronic, and a sudden worsening on top of it is acute on chronic heart failure, the last step of the sequence from pre-heart failure through exacerbation. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which statement captures the etiology of all heart failure?","choices":["Every case has a cardiomyopathy etiology","Every case begins with valve disease","Every case begins with coronary disease","Every case begins with hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All heart failure, as a syndrome, has a cardiomyopathy behind it, whether ischemic or non-ischemic. That is why the causes are sorted as ischemic cardiomyopathy versus everything else. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which baseline studies are obtained in a newly diagnosed hypertensive patient?","choices":["Renal function, electrolytes, glucose, lipids and urinalysis","Plasma free metanephrines in every patient","Serum creatinine alone, with nothing further","A chest radiograph and echocardiogram in every patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The panel does three jobs at once: it looks for secondary causes, measures organ damage, and establishes a baseline before drugs that alter potassium and renal function are started. The panel also carries a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, thyroid-stimulating hormone and a full blood count. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with severe tricuspid stenosis develops worsening hepatic congestion, and her team fears progression to cirrhosis. Which intervention is reserved for this situation?","choices":["Afterload reduction therapy only","Balloon valvotomy or valve repair","Mitral valve clip placement","Transcatheter aortic valve replacement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Balloon valvotomy and valve repair are reserved for tricuspid stenosis with hepatic congestion that may lead to cirrhosis, or with severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with prior heart attacks collapsed without any prodrome while climbing stairs and woke within 20 seconds. Which referral should accompany her cardiac testing?","choices":["Dietitian referral","Cardiology consultation","Physical therapy referral","Neurology consultation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Arrhythmic, structural and vascular cardiogenic syncope each pair their tests with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man had a myocardial infarction a year ago. He has no symptoms of heart failure and no diabetes, and his echocardiogram shows systolic dysfunction. Which regimen is recommended?","choices":["Dapagliflozin alone, since he has no symptoms","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","A converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker","A loop diuretic started at once"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic structural disease is stage B, pre-heart failure, where an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker are strong recommendations; a receptor blocker replaces the inhibitor if it is not tolerated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension asks which strokes he is at risk of. What should he be told?","choices":["Both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke","Ischemic stroke only","Subarachnoid hemorrhage specifically","Intracerebral hemorrhage only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension raises the risk of both mechanisms, and chronic small-vessel injury separately contributes to white matter change, lacunar infarcts and cognitive decline. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with newly diagnosed hypertension has proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising, thin skin and broad purple abdominal striae with central adiposity. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Primary aldosteronism","Cushing syndrome","Pheochromocytoma","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cluster of proximal weakness, bruising, thin skin, broad purple striae and central adiposity points to glucocorticoid excess as the secondary cause of her hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has had chest pressure at rest for two days. His electrocardiogram shows inverted T waves, and two standard troponin I levels are 0.01 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["ST-elevation infarction","Unstable angina","Stable angina","Infarction without ST elevation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pain at rest with nonspecific electrocardiogram changes, usually inverted T waves, and a normal troponin I is unstable angina. A standard troponin I at or below 0.04 ng/mL is not abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a Corrigan pulse (a very rapid upstroke followed by rapid collapse) and a de Musset sign (head bobbing with each heartbeat). Which valve lesion is most likely?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Corrigan pulse, also called a water hammer or collapsing pulse, and the de Musset sign are findings of chronic aortic regurgitation, along with a diastolic blowing murmur and a displaced point of maximal impulse. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from diabetes asks what she can do herself to stop fainting when she gets out of bed. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Stand quickly to train her reflexes","Stay in bed until symptoms stop","Drink less water in the evening","Change position slowly when rising"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, with a pause sitting before standing, alongside treatment of the underlying cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which setting is typical of micturition syncope?","choices":["Voiding while standing at night","Shock at hearing bad news","Exertion while climbing stairs","Diarrhea for several days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal urination after abruptly standing from bed, voiding in the standing position, triggers an exaggerated autonomic reflex with vagal activity and reduced vascular tone. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with 20 years of poorly controlled hypertension has ankle swelling that has recently spread to crackles at both lung bases. Blood pressure is 176/98. A fourth heart sound (a sound in late diastole just before the first heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows thick left ventricular walls with an ejection fraction of 62%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","Systolic failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from thyroid excess","Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diastolic chain is complete: hypertension driving hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a fourth heart sound from atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, peripheral then pulmonary edema, and a preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why is a beta blocker the wrong first choice for an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart?","choices":["The first drug is a sodium-glucose inhibitor","Beta blockers are banned in all heart failure","Diabetes makes any drug unnecessary","A loop diuretic must be started first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stage A the only strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl began isotretinoin for severe acne three months ago. Her triglyceride has risen from 120 to 410 mg/dL, with normal thyroid and kidney function. Which is the most likely cause of the rise?","choices":["Hypercholesterolemia","Anabolic steroids","Familial chylomicronemia","Isotretinoin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin, used for acne, can elevate triglyceride, as can estrogen, glucocorticoids, beta blockers and bexarotene. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has echocardiogram-confirmed pulmonic stenosis that is mild. She has no symptoms and asks about her outlook. Which is the most appropriate counseling?","choices":["She will need lifelong warfarin","Balloon valvuloplasty is needed now","No treatment; survival is excellent","Survival is about two years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis needs no treatment, and survival is 94% at 20 years. Intervention is reserved for severe disease with symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which drug is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist for a mechanical valve?","choices":["Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, the long-term anticoagulant for mechanical heart valves; if surgery requires heparin instead, warfarin is bridged until the ratio is therapeutic. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with right ventricular infarction (ST elevation in V4R) is given sublingual nitroglycerin in triage, and her blood pressure falls to 70/40 mmHg. What explains the fall?","choices":["Ventricular aneurysm","Lost preload","Myocardial rupture","Thrombolytic bleeding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin reduces preload, which removes the Frank-Starling effect the injured right ventricle depends on. Output drops further, the right ventricle fails and the patient goes into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after taking a large quantity of a relative's blood pressure pills. His pressure dropped suddenly over the past hour and he is dizzy. How is his hypotension best classified?","choices":["Acute hypotension from a toxic cause","Chronic hypotension from a cardiac cause","Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medication-mediated overdose is a toxic cause of acute hypotension, which is of sudden onset, usually symptomatic and tied to a specific trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 68-year-old smoker with peripheral arterial disease has blood pressure of 168/94 despite three agents. An abdominal bruit is audible, ultrasound shows a right kidney 2 cm smaller than the left, and he has had two episodes of sudden pulmonary edema. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Obstructive sleep apnea","Primary aldosteronism","Renovascular disease","Chronic kidney disease from diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cluster is characteristic: resistant pressure, an abdominal bruit, asymmetric kidneys and recurrent flash pulmonary edema. In an older patient with established vascular disease the mechanism is atherosclerotic stenosis, often bilateral and progressive. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy with confirmed homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has total cholesterol of 610 mg/dL on a high-intensity statin with ezetimibe. What is the treatment of choice for his disorder?","choices":["Very low-fat diet alone for a year","Low-density lipoprotein apheresis","Add a fibrate for his triglyceride","Switch to a moderate-intensity statin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is the treatment of choice, like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation. Most patients also need several drugs, starting with high-intensity statins. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman wore a defibrillator vest for three months after an infarction, with optimized guideline-directed therapy and cardiac rehabilitation. Her repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 28%. She is class II and expected to live many years. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Heart transplant evaluation now","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","Stop therapy and recheck in a year","Continue the vest indefinitely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pathway ends with an implantable device when the repeat echocardiogram shows no improvement. She also meets the criteria: ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III, optimal therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with microvascular angina asks which factor tends to go along with her episodes. Her coronary arteries were normal on angiography. What should she be told?","choices":["Cocaine or amphetamine use","Triptan migraine therapy","Stress or anxiety","The hours after midnight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is more frequent in women and is often associated with stress or anxiety. Proposed mechanisms include endothelial dysfunction, insulin resistance and estrogen deficiency. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has had left-sided heart failure with reduced ejection fraction for five years. Over two months she has developed leg edema, jugular venous distension (distended neck veins), an enlarged liver, nausea and poor appetite. Which is the most likely explanation for her new findings?","choices":["Primary liver disease with ascites","Right-sided failure from her left-sided failure","Worsening systolic failure of the left only","High-output failure from increased demand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic left-sided failure is the most common cause of right-sided failure. Systemic overload gives edema and distended neck veins; portal overload gives nausea, poor appetite and hepatomegaly. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who emigrated from a country where rheumatic fever is common has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). A diastolic rumble is heard at the apex. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Electrocardiogram only","No test until symptoms worsen","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of mitral stenosis, such as dyspnea on exertion or orthopnea, should get an echocardiogram. It shows a large left atrium and the stenotic mitral valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 43, 39"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation?","choices":["Endocarditis from intravenous drug use","Rheumatic scarring of the leaflets","Pulmonary hypertension dilating the annulus","Carcinoid disease of the valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is most commonly due to dilation of the tricuspid annulus from right ventricular dilation in the setting of pulmonary hypertension. Primary disease is infective endocarditis in drug users. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Lead V4 belongs to which territory?","choices":["Septal","Inferior","Anterior","Lateral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. V1 and V2 are septal and V3 and V4 anterior, while V5 and V6 join I and aVL as lateral. Read from V3 to V1 the groups spell A-S-S, and from V4 to V6 A-L-L. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"How do hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism differ in their effect on blood pressure?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism raises systolic; hypothyroidism raises diastolic","Hyperthyroidism raises vascular resistance and hypothyroidism raises output","Neither affects blood pressure","Both raise systolic pressure with tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the heart rate separates them further: tachycardia accompanies the systolic pattern and bradycardia the diastolic one, so the pulse points to which is operating. Hyperthyroidism does it by raising cardiac output, hypothyroidism by raising vascular resistance. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man fainted at home and has recovered. His electrocardiogram and glucose are normal. Which tests look for an infectious or metabolic contributor?","choices":["An echocardiogram and stress test","A tilt-table test and a log","A blood count and metabolic panel","Orthostatic vitals only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unremarkable blood counts and a metabolic panel lower suspicion of an infectious or metabolic source, as glucose and the electrocardiogram address hypoglycemia and dysrhythmia. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has new chest pain at rest, T-wave inversion on his electrocardiogram and a standard troponin I of 0.01 ng/mL. What does this troponin I result indicate?","choices":["It is not abnormal","It confirms an infarction","It needs no repeat draw","It is abnormal for a man"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A standard troponin I is abnormal above 0.04 ng/mL, so 0.01 ng/mL is not abnormal. Rest pain with nonspecific T-wave changes and a normal troponin is unstable angina, and the troponin I is still repeated. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with a sedentary job has a triglyceride of 280 mg/dL, low-density lipoprotein of 165 mg/dL and non-high-density cholesterol of 205 mg/dL. He has no xanthomas. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 150 with low-density lipoprotein above 130 or non-high-density above 160 defines mixed dyslipidemia, the most common type in clinical practice. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and peripheral neuropathy has had two days of shortness of breath, nausea and fatigue, with no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her and send her home","Obtain a stat electrocardiogram","Order an outpatient stress test","Treat as viral gastroenteritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chest discomfort may be absent in silent ischemia, which occurs with diabetes, peripheral neuropathy and a transplanted heart. Dyspnea, nausea and fatigue can be the presentation, and every chest pain workup starts with a stat electrocardiogram. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a normal echocardiogram asks whether she should take metoprolol, as her father did, to prevent heart failure. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Dapagliflozin comes first, not a beta blocker","A loop diuretic is the preventive drug","Metoprolol is the first drug for prevention","No drug helps until symptoms appear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart she is stage A, where the first recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin or empagliflozin. A beta blocker enters at stage B. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension and atrial fibrillation is on metoprolol. A colleague suggests adding verapamil for rate control and blood pressure. What is the concern?","choices":["Dependent ankle edema","Hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury","Severe bradycardia and heart block","Rebound hypertension on withdrawal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both agents slow the sinus and atrioventricular nodes, so combining them stacks the same effect. Both are also avoided where the ejection fraction is already reduced. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed resistant hypertension has had his diuretic optimized and his other two agents titrated to maximally tolerated doses. His filtration rate and potassium are normal. Which fourth agent has the most evidence?","choices":["Hydralazine","Spironolactone","A beta blocker","A second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, provided the filtration rate and potassium allow. It is added after the foundational three are optimized rather than instead of doing so. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman without diabetes has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 11% and an uncertain statin decision. She has never smoked and has no family history of premature disease. Her coronary artery calcium score is zero. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start a high-intensity statin","Order a skin biopsy","Add ezetimibe now","Withhold or delay the statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A calcium score of zero lowers risk, so statin therapy may be withheld or delayed, except in cigarette smokers, diabetes or a strong family history of premature disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is ready for discharge after an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His heart rate is 78/min with no bradycardia. Which drug class improves survival rates across the board after infarction?","choices":["Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitor","Beta blocker","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Calcium channel blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers improve survival rates across the board after myocardial infarction, so they belong in post-infarction management unless bradycardia or another contraindication is present. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which is a coronary heart disease risk equivalent?","choices":["Low dietary fiber","Borderline triglyceride of 170","Isolated high-density lipoprotein of 65","Diabetes mellitus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coronary risk equivalents include diabetes mellitus, peripheral arterial disease, abdominal aortic aneurysm, symptomatic carotid disease and a Framingham risk above 20%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a mid-systolic click at the apex. An echocardiogram is ordered. Which finding confirms the suspected valve problem?","choices":["A calcified, narrowed aortic valve","Thick, redundant mitral leaflets","A dilated tricuspid annulus","A dilated aortic root"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram showing thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm, flaring into the left atrium during systole, from myxomatous degeneration. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction asks why his clinician raises one medicine at a time rather than starting everything at full strength. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Only one drug can be taken at once","Medicines stop once symptoms go","Therapy is optimized as tolerated","Full doses are unsafe in any patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed therapy is prioritized for his comorbidities and optimized as hemodynamically stable, so each class is increased as blood pressure and tolerance allow. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which test shows a regional wall motion abnormality?","choices":["Echocardiogram","Troponin I level","Calcium score","Chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Regional wall motion abnormalities (hypokinesia, akinesia, dyskinesia) are seen on an echocardiogram, which images the moving walls of the ventricle segment by segment. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 35"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is newly diagnosed with heart failure and will start a loop diuretic. Which laboratory panel should be checked before starting?","choices":["Complete metabolic panel","Coagulation panel","Hepatic function panel","Thyroid function panel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The metabolic panel gives potassium and renal function (blood urea nitrogen, creatinine and filtration rate), both of which a potassium-wasting loop diuretic affects. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome after her heart rate rose by 38 within 10 minutes of standing. Which is the most appropriate first treatment?","choices":["Increase water and salt intake","Start midodrine","Begin vasopressin","Restrict salt and fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increasing water and salt intake is the first listed treatment, with fludrocortisone as the drug best suited to the syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
